  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(3)+{f}^{\u2032}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-3)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(3) & =-\\frac{1}{(3)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-3)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(3)}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{9})=-\\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
